# =================================================== #


Table: Products

+------------------+---------+
| Column Name      | Type    |
+------------------+---------+
| product_id       | int     |
| product_name     | varchar |
| product_category | varchar |
+------------------+---------+

product_id is the primary key (column with unique values) for this table.

This table contains data about the company's products.
 

Table: Orders

+---------------+---------+
| Column Name   | Type    |
+---------------+---------+
| product_id    | int     |
| order_date    | date    |
| unit          | int     |
+---------------+---------+

This table may have duplicate rows.

product_id is a foreign key (reference column) to the Products table.

unit is the number of products ordered in order_date.
 

Write a solution to get the names of products that have at least 100 units ordered in February 2020 and their amount.

Return the result table in any order.


# =================================================== #
                      SOLUTION
# =================================================== #


import pandas as pd

def list_products(products: pd.DataFrame, orders: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame: 

    time_mask = (orders['order_date'] >= '2020-02-01') & (orders['order_date'] < '2020-03-01')

    product_sum = orders[time_mask].groupby(by = 'product_id', as_index = False)['unit'].sum()

    product_sum = product_sum[product_sum['unit'] >= 100]

    product_sum['product_name'] = product_sum['product_id'].map(products.set_index('product_id')['product_name'])

    return product_sum.loc[:, ['product_name', 'unit']]
